**Verdict:** VVS1 is the ceiling for practical clarity at 1ct. Above it, you're paying for the certificate grade, not for a better-looking stone. If VVS1 at $886 fits the budget, it's the strongest clarity you can justify visually. See our [marquise diamond clarity guide](/marquise-diamond-clarity-guide) for the complete framework.

> **"I recommend stopping at VVS1 for any lab stone under 1.5ct. IF and FL cost significantly more and look identical. The only reason to pay for IF or FL at 1ct is if you're buying for the certificate — for resale documentation, for a gift where the paperwork matters, or for personal satisfaction. None of those are wrong reasons. But if you're optimising for what the ring looks like on your hand, VVS1 is where you stop."** — Farzana Hasan, Diamond Expert

## The D-vs-E False Economy

**The D-vs-E False Economy is the mistake of paying for D colour over E colour at 1ct when the visual difference is undetectable under normal conditions.** At 1ct lab marquise, E-VVS1 costs $886. D-VVS1 costs $958. The $72 difference buys you one colour grade on the certificate. Nothing more.

Here is the colour scale at 1ct lab marquise, in plain terms:

- **D colour:** Completely colourless. The absolute top. Zero warmth visible in any light under any conditions.
- **E colour:** Essentially colourless. One grade below D. Zero warmth visible to the naked eye. Even trained graders can only detect the difference under controlled lab conditions.
- **F colour:** Essentially colourless. Two grades below D. Still no visible tint under normal viewing.

D vs E at 1ct in a real ring: you cannot tell them apart. A gemologist comparing both side by side in controlled lighting may detect a trace difference. In a setting, on a finger, in daylight or indoor lighting — the stones look identical.

**Where the false economy kicks in:** jump from $629 E-VVS2 to $958 D-VVS1 and you spend $329 extra. You get a better colour grade (E→D) and a better clarity grade (VVS2→VVS1). Neither step changes the visual appearance. You're buying a stronger certificate, not a better-looking stone.

**When D is worth it:** if you're setting the stone in platinum and you want the absolute maximum certificate for documentation purposes — or if D matters to you personally — then $958 for D-VVS1 is a reasonable choice. The stone is genuinely the best-certified 1ct lab marquise under $1,000. It just doesn't look better than the $629 stone.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Colour</th><th>Grade Description</th><th>Visible in Setting</th><th>Price at VVS1</th><th>Cost to Upgrade from E</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td>Colourless</td><td>No tint visible</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29978545?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$958</a></td><td>+$72 vs E-VVS1</td><td>Best cert. Same eye appearance.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>E (SWEET SPOT)</strong></td><td>Essentially colourless</td><td>No tint visible</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29517802?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$886</a></td><td>Baseline</td><td>Our colour pick. Saves $72 vs D.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>F</strong></td><td>Essentially colourless</td><td>No tint visible</td><td>~$820</td><td>Saves ~$66 vs E</td><td>Lower cert, same eye look. Not in this dataset.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>G</strong></td><td>Near-colourless</td><td>Trace warmth in white gold</td><td>~$700</td><td>Saves ~$186 vs E</td><td>Yellow/rose gold setting only.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** E is the colour sweet spot at 1ct lab marquise. D adds $72 for a certificate upgrade with no visual return. F saves $66 vs E with no visible difference. In yellow or rose gold, step down to G and save ~$186 more — the warm metal makes E–D colour redundant. See our [marquise diamond colour guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide).

> **"The D-vs-E question comes up constantly. My answer is always the same: if you're buying E-VVS1 at $886, you're already getting a stone that reads identically to D-VVS1 on the finger. The $72 difference is for the letter on the certificate. I've set hundreds of E and D stones side by side in platinum. Every client who sees them together confirms they cannot tell them apart. Save the $72 or put it toward a better setting."** — Farzana Hasan, Diamond Expert

## Grade 1 — E-VVS2 at $629 (Entry Pick)

The E-VVS2 Excellent is the entry floor for 1ct certified lab marquise in current inventory. Three stones available at $629. All IGI-certified. All Excellent cut grade.

**What you get at $629:** E colour (essentially colourless, indistinguishable from D under normal viewing), VVS2 clarity (inclusions not visible without deliberate 10x effort), Excellent cut (the second-highest cut grade on the IGI scale — adequate for marquise where Ideal and Excellent both screen out the worst bow-tie risk). Face-up: approximately 10x5mm at 1ct.

**What the natural comparison looks like:** A natural 1ct G-VS2 marquise costs ~$3,000. G colour has a trace warmth that E does not have. VS2 clarity has inclusions that VVS2 does not have. The lab stone is a better grade at 79% less.

**When to choose E-VVS2:** You want the maximum lab stone value per dollar. You're setting in yellow or rose gold (where E vs D doesn't matter). You plan to pair the stone with a setting in the $1,200–$2,000 range and want to keep the total ring under $2,000. You understand the certificate grade and know VS2 or VVS2 doesn't affect the look.

