## Pros and Cons — 1ct Lab Grown Marquise Diamond

**Pros:**
- **79%–84% savings vs natural** at every grade tier from E-VVS2 to D-VVS1.
- **3 grades under $1,000** — E-VVS2, E-VVS1, D-VVS1. All IGI-certified.
- **Same chemical structure as natural** — identical hardness (10 Mohs), brilliance, and face-up size.
- **Higher grade accessible** — D-VVS1 for $958 vs $6,000 natural. The lab budget buys better certificates.
- **Elongation advantage** — 1ct marquise measures ~10x5mm face-up. That reads larger than a 1ct round (6.5mm). Lab lets you compare all L:W ratios without scarcity premium.
- **Complete rings under $2,200** — $629 stone + $1,180 platinum setting = $1,809 total ring.

**Cons:**
- **Low resale value** — lab prices continue to fall. A $629 lab stone today may sell for $100–$150 in five years. Not an investment.
- **No GCAL option at 1ct** — GCAL 8X guarantee inventory starts at 1.25ct. All 1ct lab marquise in current inventory is IGI only.
- **Bow-tie risk still applies** — lab or natural, a poorly proportioned marquise has a bow-tie shadow. Always watch the 360° video. Excellent/Ideal cut grades help screen but don't guarantee. See our [marquise diamond bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).
- **Certificate verification required** — always check the IGI certificate number at [igi.org](https://www.igi.org/) before purchase.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a 1 carat lab grown marquise diamond?

A 1 carat lab grown marquise diamond is a 1.00ct marquise-shaped diamond grown in a reactor (CVD or HPHT) rather than mined. The stone is chemically and physically identical to a natural 1ct marquise — same carbon structure, same 10 Mohs hardness, same refractive index. The only differences are origin and price. IGI certifies all the stones in this guide on the same scale as natural diamonds.

### How much does a 1 carat lab grown marquise diamond cost?

**$629 to $1,381** in current certified inventory. The 3 grades under $1,000: E-VVS2 Excellent at $629, E-VVS1 Excellent at $886, D-VVS1 Ideal at $958. Above $1,000, D-IF and D-FL Ideal both sit at $1,381. A natural 1ct G-VS2 marquise costs ~$3,000 — the lab entry saves 79%.

### What are the 3 grades under $1,000 for a 1ct lab marquise?

**E-VVS2 at $629, E-VVS1 at $886, D-VVS1 at $958.** All three are IGI-certified, eye-clean, and Excellent or Ideal cut. The steps between them are certificate upgrades — the stones look identical on the finger. Choose by how much the grade documentation matters to you personally.

### Is E or D colour better for a 1ct lab marquise?

**E is the practical sweet spot.** D costs $72 more at VVS1 for a colour difference undetectable under normal conditions. In white gold or platinum, D and E read identically white. In yellow or rose gold, step down to G and save more. D is worth the extra $72 only if the certificate grade matters to you or if you're setting in platinum and want the absolute maximum documentation.

### Is VVS2 or VVS1 better at 1ct?

**Visually identical — VVS2 is the value choice.** Both are completely eye-clean. Both look the same on the finger. VVS1 has a stronger clarity grade on the certificate. The $257 step from E-VVS2 ($629) to E-VVS1 ($886) buys a cleaner grade, not a better-looking stone. Choose VVS1 if the certificate matters; choose VVS2 if you're optimising value.

### Is IF or FL worth paying for at 1ct?

**No — not for visual quality.** D-IF and D-FL at 1ct both cost $1,381, which is $423 more than D-VVS1. Both look identical to D-VVS1 on the finger. No naked-eye or standard loupe difference exists between VVS1 and IF at 1ct. Pay for IF/FL only if the absolute maximum clarity grade on the certificate has personal or documentation value to you. See the VVS1 Value Ceiling section above.

### What does a 1ct marquise diamond look like face-up?

A 1ct marquise measures approximately 10x5mm face-up — a long, narrow pointed oval. It has the largest face-up area of any standard diamond cut at 1ct, roughly 15–20% larger than a 1ct round (6.5mm). The elongation creates a finger-slimming effect. The ideal L:W ratio is 1.95–2.05. See our [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

### What L:W ratio should I target for a 1ct lab marquise?

**1.95–2.05.** This is the classic marquise proportion — elongated enough for the finger-slimming effect, not so long that the tips become fragile. Below 1.85, the stone looks stubby. Above 2.10, tip-chip risk increases. With multiple stones at the same price in this inventory, compare all options and select the one closest to your target L:W. See our full [marquise diamond length to width ratio guide](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio).

### Does a 1ct lab marquise have a bow-tie?

**It can — bow-tie is a cut quality issue, not a lab/natural issue.** A poorly proportioned marquise of any origin has a dark shadow across the belly from light leakage. Selecting IGI Excellent or Ideal cut grades filters out the worst cases. But the only reliable test is watching the stone's 360° video on the retailer's product page. Always do this before purchasing any marquise diamond. See our [marquise diamond bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).

### What setting is best for a 1ct lab grown marquise?

**Six-prong solitaire or bezel for tip protection.** The marquise's pointed ends are the most chip-vulnerable part of the stone. A six-prong setting with prongs at each tip is the standard protection. A bezel setting fully wraps both tips for maximum security — better for active lifestyles. For sparkle, the petite pavé solitaire adds shoulder stones without overpowering a 1ct centre. See our [marquise solitaire engagement ring guide](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring) and [marquise bezel engagement ring guide](/marquise-bezel-engagement-ring).

### Can I get a GCAL certificate on a 1ct lab marquise?

**Not in current inventory.** GCAL 8X guarantee inventory for lab marquise starts at 1.25ct. All 1ct lab marquise stones in this guide are IGI-certified. IGI is fully reliable at 1ct — verify the certificate number at [igi.org](https://www.igi.org/) before purchase. GCAL becomes relevant at 1.5ct+ where the grade-certainty premium starts to justify the cost.

### What is the cheapest complete ring with a 1ct lab marquise?

**Around $1,879** — pairing the E-VVS2 at $629 with a Comfort Fit Bezel Setting in white gold at $1,250. A six-prong classic solitaire at $1,355 gives the second-cheapest complete ring at $1,984. Both are under $2,000 for a certified 1ct lab marquise engagement ring.

### Does a 1ct lab marquise hold its value?

**No.** Lab diamond prices have declined significantly since 2021 and continue to fall as production scales. A $629 lab stone today may be worth $100–$150 on resale in five years. Buy it as a permanent piece you love wearing, not as a store of value. Natural 1ct marquise holds resale value better — but even natural engagement rings typically sell at a loss.

### What colour grade should I choose in yellow gold for a 1ct lab marquise?

**G colour.** In yellow gold, the warm metal fills in any colour tint from the stone — the difference between G and D becomes invisible. G lab marquise stones save $150–$200 vs E at the same clarity. In white gold or platinum, E is the smart choice. See our [marquise diamond colour guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide).

### How many 1ct lab grown marquise stones are available?

The data in this guide covers 17 certified IGI stones at 1.00ct specifically, from $629 to $1,381. The [full certified lab marquise inventory](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-diamonds/marquise-cut-diamonds?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) includes 313 total stones across all sizes. Filter by carat weight to isolate the 1ct tier and compare 360° videos.

### Is a 1ct lab marquise a good choice for an engagement ring?

**Yes — it's an outstanding value.** At $629–$958 for a certified D-E colour, VVS1-VVS2 stone, a 1ct lab marquise delivers a large face-up diamond for well under $1,000. The complete ring stays under $2,200. Natural 1ct equivalent rings start at $4,000+. See the [lab grown marquise diamond guide](/lab-grown-marquise-diamond) for the full size-by-size savings comparison.

